    "summary": "High‑energy Winterland with stellar They Love Each Other and Eyes of the World",
    "blurb": "The set showcases a soaring rendition of They Love Each Other, a seamless Playing In The Band → Bertha transition, and an inspired Eyes Of The World. Jerry’s leads cut through with crystal‑clear tone while Phil anchors the jams with thumping bass. Keith’s piano adds texture throughout the second‑set marathon.",
    "review": "Winterland on November 9, 1973 finds the Dead riding a high‑energy wave from the opening number straight into a string of unforgettable moments. After a solid first set that builds momentum, the band erupts in the second set: Here Comes Sunshine opens with Jerry’s “insane” solo work, and the group rolls into a marathon of tightly linked tunes—Me And My Uncle, To Lay Me Down, Big River, then a soaring segue from Greatest Story Ever Told into Bertha. The centerpiece is an expansive Eyes Of The World that showcases Phil’s harmonic bass wizardry and Jerry’s loopy, soaring leads, while Keith’s piano adds just the right color. The night closes with a joyous encore of Uncle John’s Band, leaving the audience buzzing long after the lights came up.",
